<main id="main">
  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="hero-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-doccheck"/></svg></span>
      <p class="kicker">Guide</p>
      <h1>Can I sell my company&rsquo;s data?</h1>
      <p class="lead">Sometimes. Owning the records is not the same as being allowed to license them, so the answer usually comes down to three questions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Three questions that decide it</h2>
      <ol class="path reveal">
        <li>
          <h3>Do you hold the rights?</h3>
          <p>Records your staff create in your systems are usually yours to license, subject to your contracts. Records a customer, supplier or platform created may not be.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Does anything prohibit it?</h3>
          <p>Customer agreements, confidentiality clauses, tool terms and sector rules can each limit or rule out licensing. All of them are read before anything is shared.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Is personal data involved?</h3>
          <p>If people are identifiable in the records, that information gets its own review. Removing names is not the same as making data anonymous.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="reveal">
        <h2>Records your company creates</h2>
        <p>Most of what AI teams want is ordinary work product: how a problem was described, what was tried, what was decided and what happened next.</p>
        <ul class="examples"><li>Support tickets</li><li>Internal project threads</li><li>Playbooks and revisions</li><li>Deal notes</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <h2>Records someone else created</h2>
        <p>These need a closer look, because your right to license them may sit with the other party.</p>
        <ul class="examples"><li>Customer-authored messages</li><li>Supplier and vendor records</li><li>Third-party research</li><li>Data you license but do not own</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What is checked before a buyer sees anything</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Customer agreements</strong>Whether they restrict sharing or licensing, and whether notice is required.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool terms</strong>Some platforms limit reuse of content your team creates inside them.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>Client and third-party confidences, including professional privilege.</li>
        <li><strong>Personal data</strong>Who appears in the records, and what has to be removed or transformed.</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership</strong>Whether contractors, subsidiaries or partners hold any part of it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small-note">This page is general information, not legal advice. Your own counsel reviews any agreement before you sign it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Questions people ask first</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <details><summary>Do I need my customers&rsquo; permission?</summary><p>It depends on your contracts and on what is in the records. Where notice or consent is required, that is established before any buyer sees the data.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if my tools&rsquo; terms do not allow it?</summary><p>Then that content is not licensed. The scope can often be narrowed to the records that are clear, rather than abandoning the whole dataset.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does removing names make it safe?</summary><p>No. Anonymisation is a legal standard, not a formatting step. Personal information gets its own review.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">The clauses that actually decide it</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Rights questions are rarely answered by one document. They are answered by four, and the fourth is the one people forget.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <caption>Where to look, and what each document controls</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Document</th><th scope="col">What it usually says</th><th scope="col">What it means for licensing</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Employment and contractor terms</td><td>Work created by staff in the course of their job belongs to the company; contractor work may not.</td><td>Clears the records your own team wrote. Contractor-authored material needs its own check.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer agreement</td><td>Customer information is used to provide the service, and kept confidential.</td><td>This is the usual blocker. Purpose limitation is what rules licensing out, not ownership.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tool terms of service</td><td>You keep your content; the platform gets a license to operate and improve its service.</td><td>The platform&rsquo;s license to itself is not a license to you. It neither grants nor removes your right to license.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sector rules</td><td>Professional obligations, privilege, financial or health privacy rules.</td><td>Can remove whole categories from scope regardless of what your contracts say.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">The second row is why &ldquo;we own our data&rdquo; is not an answer. A company can own a record and still be unable to license it, because the customer agreement limited what the record may be used for.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Where the line usually falls</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Three distinctions cover most of the cases we see. They are not legal tests, but they predict the answer well enough to plan around.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Your staff&rsquo;s own work product</strong>Internal threads, resolution notes, playbooks and deal notes your team wrote. Usually the clearest ground, subject to the customer agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer-authored content</strong>What the customer wrote to you. Readable by you, but licensing it to a third party is a different act from reading it.</li>
        <li><strong>Material you license but do not own</strong>Third-party research, purchased datasets, syndicated content. Your rights end where your license ends.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">A scope can mix these. Excluding the second and third categories and licensing the first is a normal, narrower deal, and it is often the one that closes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a narrow scope looks like</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The most useful thing a seller can bring to a first call is not a bigger dataset. It is a clear edge: what is in, what is out, and why.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">In scope</th><th scope="col">Out of scope</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Internal resolution notes and fixes</td><td>Customer messages</td><td>Keeps the reasoning, drops the customer&rsquo;s words.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Playbooks and revision history</td><td>Client deliverables</td><td>Deliverables may belong to the client, not the firm.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deal notes with reasons recorded</td><td>Names, emails, pricing terms</td><td>The commercial logic survives de-identification; the parties do not.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">None of this is legal advice, and it is not a substitute for your own counsel reading the agreement. It is the map we use to decide what to ask them about.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">More questions</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <details><summary>Does owning the data mean I can license it?</summary><p>No. Ownership and permitted use are separate. A company can own a record and still be barred from licensing it by the customer agreement that governs it.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if only part of the dataset is clear?</summary><p>Then that part is the dataset. Narrowing the scope is normal and usually faster than trying to clear everything.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I have to tell my customers?</summary><p>It depends on your agreement and where they are. Where notice or consent is required, that is settled before a buyer sees anything.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is a license the same as a sale?</summary><p>No. A license grants defined rights for a defined term and purpose; ownership stays with you. That distinction is what makes most of these deals possible at all.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

